# WpfPilot + target app workflow

WpfPilot is under active development. When using it to drive a target WPF app (e.g. EquipLink `EL_UI`):

## Extend WpfPilot when needed

If the target app exposes a gap (menu navigation, window state, screenshots while minimized, elevated lifecycle, etc.), **add the feature to WpfPilot** rather than working around it in one-off scripts.

- Keep changes minimal and reusable for any WPF app.
- Add or update unit/integration tests for new behavior.
- Run `dotnet test` and ensure **all existing tests pass** before finishing.
- **Check in WpfPilot** (`git commit`) when WpfPilot code changed — do not leave WpfPilot edits uncommitted.

## Driving the target app

- Launch with `WPFPILOT_START_MINIMIZED=1` (CLI edit loop does this) so the agent/IDE stays visible; use `bring_to_front` only when a human must see the window.
- Screenshots via `RenderTargetBitmap` work while minimized — prefer that over keeping the app foregrounded.
- For elevated targets (`requireAdministrator`), run Cursor/CLI elevated so start/stop works without UAC prompts.

## Target app changes

UI fixes belong in the **target app repo** (e.g. `EL_UI`), not in WpfPilot. WpfPilot only gets automation/protocol improvements.
